00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.309 align:start position:0%
 
Michael I want to just jump right in and  start by asking about the inspiration  behind the underlying concepts and  theories in this film what are the  origins of these things the inspiration  for the film and themes that comes a  long way I worked on this movie for the  better part of eight years with my  co-writer and collaborator Ben neighbors  and I think you know we early on talked  about our own relationship with sound  both individuals who move to New York  City and you know weren't from there  originally and I think we we thought  about you know the way sound affected us  and played into our lives on a daily  basis a lot and I think I think whether  or not people realize it or not it's  this thing worse we all have a  relationship with sound and a lot of  times subconsciously and I think we were  both we're not scientists but we're  interested in science we're interested  in music and we wanted to explore those  things through this character and it  started through this idea for this house  turner character and we thought he was  this great kind of conduit to tell a  story about sound and yeah I just I said  I suppose our interest in the way sound  is playing into our lives this is based  on a short film so talk to us about the  journey that was an award jury  award-winning film in 2013 called pan  obsessed and it went on to do dozens of  other festivals what was that journey  like from short to feature well it's um  it's it's it's a long journey I first  first started working it was in 2011 I  was in California when I first read the  idea that Ben had sent me of kind of an  outline for a short story called  palimpsest and it had this character of  this house turner Anna and I really like  this character it sounded that I could  picture you know him wearing a tweed  blazer and carrying a satchel and I love  the idea of someone kind of walking  around with musical instrument equipment  but wasn't a musician and so that was  the germ of the idea and adapted that  into a screenplay and turned it into a  short film just for a short film I  really liked that medium but it was  never intended to be anything else and  we played it back in 2013 I played at  Sundance and I  we saw that there was you know it was  resonating with audiences there was a  conversation around it and it got kind  of been and I organically thinking about  what can we do we have a bigger story  here we like this character is there can  we kind of build more around his world  and we took a year it up you know  drafted out as a feature and I'd like to  say that we just jumped in and made the  movie but you know somehow six or seven  years pass and it takes a long time to  convince I suppose the the powers that  be the the financier is that it's it's  worthy to tell a movie about a somewhat  kind of you know I know didactic  scientist who lives in a basement and as  obsessed with sound so it's it was a  learning you know moment for me to  understand kind of you know what what  works on the page and how to convince  people but you know fortunately with  that time you got to develop and iron  things out more which I I never think is  a bad thing not it may be a sustainable  practice as a filmmaker and to make a  career but you know when all is said and  done it's it's it's part of making the  movie but a very long process and then  we finally found you know like-minded  people and sometime during that process  I was able to get the script a Peter  Sarsgaard who I had dreamed about being  in this role for quite a long time and  to my surprise when he when we finally  did get him the script and was attached  with a love letter that I wrote him it  was within a couple days that we were  sitting down and talking about the role  and I found at him you know this kind of  kindred spirit to the character that he  was playing someone as an actor and  human being just obsessed with sound and  considers himself to have a perfect  pitch or absolute pitch and you know  then suddenly once you start getting  stars attached everything kind of starts  moving along very quickly well in terms  of that score and the dovetailing of all  these elements because I mean we've got  score we've got sound design we also got  sound cues which I want to talk about  but the scores so dreamlike in spare and  it's yeah and he had very nuanced and  and occasionally very jolting and it for  me it references it kind of is an oral  reference to the concepts in the film  and also has a very specific way of  evoking New York  which I thought was really very striking  can you talk about how you achieved all  of that well we I guess in that very  very early conversation we had talked a  lot about early electronic music like  early 20th century composers and we  talked about jazz we talked about Peters  character and he has this very evolved  sense of music and and it's very  advanced and intellectual but at the  same time he's very precise and  methodical so I guess the the first  thing that I instinctually I just  started writing very kind of intricate  complex stuff and then ultimately taking  a sledgehammer to all of it and powering  it away to its most simple form is its  simplest state and that was one of the  joys of the collaboration I think was  really like getting to the heart of who  he is as a you know that there's  something about you know trying to  reference all of these sort of  specifically very highly evolved  intellectual musical ideas but also not  just like overwhelm you with that so to  be spared seemed to be quite important  but yeah we talked a lot about the  theremin and the instrument that's  really featured the most and the score  is actually a on des martinet which is a  1920s French electronic instrument  there's kind of like a very precise  theremin you it's like on a key bed and  you put your finger on it's on a wire  and you put your finger in a ring and  you can kind of play the it's very  precise and you play the velocity with  your left hand and with your right hand  you're kind of going up and down the  keyboard with this thing and it just  seemed to really work for him to have  that kind of it's very haunting but it's  also something kind of intricate and I  don't know it's like a plain song  sometimes as well what was the  experience of working with the cast like  that I mean you have performances from  Austin Pendleton Tony Romo Laurie or  rashida Jones Peter skarsgård I've been  I know you said that Peter was the first  one to the cast how did this all come  together and what was it like working  with these guys yeah I mean I guess I'm  lucky that it I was able to get such a  great cast you know  on my first feature I I suppose had a  lot of passion to get each one of them  specifically they were you know all  Causton Pendleton I'm glad you brought  him up he was someone who I've always  loved as a like a New York character  actor and can kind of do anything and he  had quitted coincidentally worked with  Peter he had directed Peter actually on  Broadway so they they kind of I love  that in real life they had this kind of  mentor you know relationship already and  it's you know Peter is a very generous  actor I found he's usually you know if  he has ideas it's usually to kind of  give his lines out or to cut back what  he's actually saying which I you know  really appreciate and is very modest and  and humbling to watch as an actor but  you know I think it's it's such a great  privilege to be able to you know work  with these things that you've had in  your head for so long and to finally  have it vocalized one of the first  things that Peter and I did when he came  on was we sat down in my apartment and  just recorded the entire script we had  an you know I read every single other  role and he read all of his but suddenly  it was um I realized I had this kind of  radio drama version of the movie after  that I can listen to and I just just  ended up using that as a tool and then  suddenly started out in music cues and  images on a timeline on my computer and  I I kind of felt like I could just watch  the movie and then all I had to do was  you know go out and shoot and fill in  those pieces but it was this great kind  of blueprint to have going in so I was  fortunate you know to work with actors  who were very generous with their time  and you know passionate about the work  and it was you know I say filmmaking is  often a pure form of collaboration and I  think that's certainly true with all of  the you know the cast that comes  together to do this and it's um you know  working with rashida Jones who is also  you know a filmmaker and writer in her  own right I think she's always best when  she was able to take scenes and you know  kind of meld it on the day and she's  very quick it you know being able to  kind of a depth  what kind of works best for her and so I  was really just I had a front-row seat  to be able to witness kind of these  things happening and and and in a great  way  thank you both very much I appreciate  your coming tonight

